## Expert Summary

The gap problem is real. Emerald cut solitaires have flat-base settings and most diamond wedding bands are curved. They do not sit flush.

The seven-stone step-cut band is the most direct solution — not because it's the only beautiful option, but because the geometry actually works.

Of all the emerald cut diamond wedding bands in certified inventory, the lab seven-stone at $2,120–$4,730 offers the best grade structure. F-G color and VS2-SI1 clarity puts a VS2 floor on each of the seven step-cut tables. That floor matters specifically for step-cut bands, where inclusions are individually visible rather than hidden by brilliance.

The natural bezel seven-stone at $2,962 on 25% sale is the best choice for natural-origin buyers who want daily wearability. Bezel corners protect against chips. The 4.1mm width at 1ct pairs with the widest range of solitaire settings.

All three 14K metal colors are available at identical pricing.

If you don't want a step-cut wedding band beside your emerald cut solitaire, use a thin straight-shank plain metal band in the same metal. Under 2mm, traditional-fit inner surface, no channel, no dome. That is the closest a non-step-cut band gets to sitting flush against an emerald cut solitaire without custom work.

> "The gap problem is the one thing I wish every emerald cut buyer knew before they chose a wedding band. It's not your ring's fault and it's not the band's fault — it's just geometry. The fix is to stop trying to make a round peg fit a flat hole and choose a band that was built flat to begin with." — Farzana Hasan

